  "materials-hardening-physics-review.json": {
    "source_sha256": "f7f4dbe0ab90d90ad8a569216d89f0d6e8ab43ecddf4c566c048771fca5a5edb",
    "quantum-calorimetry-r1": {
      "status": "passed_final_source_and_report_review",
      "findings": [
        "The calorimeter definition compares heat into two complete equilibrium devices at fixed mechanical and coupling controls. The blank is the identical uncoupled bath, and finite-device subtraction precedes the continuum limit. This fixes the physical observable without prescribing a correction formula or numerical solver.",
        "The stated completed-square Hamiltonian fixes the counterterm and natural frequency. The spectral density convention J=(pi/2)sum c_j^2/(m_j*omega_j)delta implies the finite-bath coupling c_j^2=2*J(omega_j)*omega_j*weight_j/pi at unit bath masses. The reference retains this exact counterterm and its stiffness is positive.",
        "The Drude susceptibility follows from the same Hamiltonian. The supplied predictor correctly computes both coordinate and momentum Gibbs covariances and their temperature derivatives; its readout is the derivative of local oscillator energy, a distinct observable from the specified excess calorimeter capacity.",
        "The phase-shift oracle uses Im[-D_prime/D]/pi with the retarded sign convention. The reference instead diagonalizes a finite coupled Hamiltonian, subtracting bare-bath oscillator heat capacities. Their agreement is independent of the local-energy approximation.",
        "Zero coupling makes the excess and local heat capacities identical at every calibration temperature. The oscillator heat capacity decreases strictly with positive natural frequency, making the sole unknown identifiable. Fixed sigma carries no noiseless-response information.",
        "The public instruction is unchanged and neutral. Public text supplies quantum statistics, Gibbs preparation, finite subtraction order, spectral normalization, and fixed controls; it does not mandate a mean-force Hamiltonian, phase-shift derivation, or specific solver. Private tests constrain the documented output and fitted parameter, not an internal implementation."
      ],
